# Local development secrets for `wrangler dev` (and vitest, which also loads .dev.vars).
# Copy to `apps/api/.dev.vars` — that file is gitignored and must never be committed.
# In production these are set with `wrangler secret put <NAME>`.
#
# Everything else (APP_NAME, TRUSTED_ORIGINS, EMAIL_FROM, E2E_EMAIL_PATTERN, SIGNUP_ALLOWLIST) lives in
# `wrangler.jsonc` under `vars`, because none of it is secret.
#
# There is deliberately NO mail-provider key here (no RESEND_API_KEY, no SMTP anything). This Worker
# cannot deliver email by design — verification and reset links land in `email_outbox` and are read back
# through `GET /internal/outbox`. See `wrangler.jsonc` and `src/infrastructure/email/log-email-sender.ts`.

# Signing key for Better Auth sessions and tokens. Any long random string; rotate it and every session
# and every outstanding reset token dies. Generate one with:
#   openssl rand -base64 32
BETTER_AUTH_SECRET="replace-me-with-openssl-rand-base64-32"

# Guards `/internal/*` — the e2e outbox reader AND `POST /internal/reset-link`, the break-glass account
# recovery path. When UNSET, those routes 404 entirely.
#
# BE AWARE: because `/internal/reset-link` mints a real password-reset token, this secret is an
# ACCOUNT-TAKEOVER CREDENTIAL for this deployment. That is an accepted trade for a single-user app whose
# owner is the sole holder of the secret and the sole account — and it is what stops the account being
# permanently unrecoverable, since this Worker cannot send mail. Do not copy the pattern into a
# multi-user product. See `docs/work/07-api-review/report.md` → "Account recovery".
#   openssl rand -hex 24
INTERNAL_SECRET="replace-me-with-openssl-rand-hex-24"
